finish('uniform-word-problem-for-one-relation-monoids',
 id='TCS-6641',title='The uniform word problem for one-relation monoids',area='Automated reasoning and unification',criterion='decision',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Is there a single algorithm that, given a finite alphabet A and four finite words u,v,s,t∈A*, always halts and decides whether s and t represent the same element of the monoid presented by ⟨A | u=v⟩?',
 definitions='A* consists of all finite strings over A, including the empty word ε, with concatenation as multiplication and ε as identity. The presentation ⟨A | u=v⟩ denotes A*/≡, where ≡ is the least equivalence relation containing u≡v that is compatible with concatenation on both sides. Equivalently, s≡t exactly when some finite sequence s=w_0,w_1,…,w_k=t exists in which each adjacent pair is xu y,xv y in either order for some x,y∈A*. The fixed words u and v are literal strings, not variables that can be substituted by arbitrary words. Empty sides are permitted: replacing ε means inserting the other side at a gap in the current string. The alphabet and all four words form a finite explicit input; one may encode A as {1,…,a} and its letters as integers. The requested algorithm is one deterministic Turing machine for all presentations and all word pairs, with no oracle and no unbounded advice. There is no polynomial-time or other prescribed resource bound, but termination is required on both equal and unequal pairs. This is an ordinary monoid presentation. No inverse operation, cancellation law, group axiom or commutativity assumption is present. The presentation itself varies with the input: the card asks for uniform decidability, not merely a separate non-effectively selected procedure for each fixed presentation.',
 answer_criterion='Construct an always-terminating uniform decision procedure with a correctness proof, or prove that the language of positive presentation-and-word-pair instances is undecidable. One explicit fixed one-relation monoid with undecidable word problem would suffice for a negative answer, but is stronger than is logically required to refute uniform decidability. Enumeration that halts only on equal words, or a procedure whose termination on unequal words is unproved, is insufficient. An exponential derivation-length lower bound is compatible with decidability.',
 context=[
 paragraph('This is a minimal string-transformation problem. There is only one reversible replacement rule, and it can be applied inside any surrounding context. Starting with a word, repeated replacements can generate many others. Equality asks whether the target lies in this reachability set. The alphabet symbols themselves have no hidden arithmetic meaning; any complicated computation would have to emerge from overlaps and repeated applications of this one rule.'),
 paragraph('Positive answers are always semidecidable. Enumerate all finite replacement sequences from s and stop if t appears. Every individual stage has finitely many choices, even when one side of the relation is empty. The missing ingredient is an effective stopping argument for negative instances. Seeing no derivation within a long search does not establish inequality when intermediate words may continue to grow.'),
 paragraph('Length-preserving relations illustrate an easy case. If |u|=|v|, every reachable word has length |s|, so exhaustive search takes place in a finite graph. Unequal input lengths immediately imply inequality. When the relation changes length, that finite-state argument disappears. The question permits enormous running times, so even an impractical computable bound on the needed search would be a meaningful resolution.'),
 paragraph('Simply orienting a rule toward shorter words need not produce unique normal forms. For the relation baba=aba, the word bababa can shorten through ababa to aaba, or directly to baaba. Neither aaba nor baaba contains baba, so both are irreducible under the shortening rule, although they are equal in the presented monoid. This example shows why termination of one chosen reduction strategy is different from a complete equality test.'),
 paragraph('The surrounding algebra offers major positive results. Magnus proved decidability for one-relator groups in 1932. For ordinary monoids, classical work handles important cases such as a single relation equating a word to the identity, and presentations satisfying appropriate cycle-free conditions. Group algorithms cannot simply be applied to every monoid presentation: adjoining inverses may identify additional words, so equality in a related group can lose information.','survey'),
 paragraph('The historical source needs its correction. Nyberg-Brodda’s survey traces the string-rewriting background to Thue, while its 2022 corrigendum identifies Novikov’s 1957 question as the earliest explicit formulation known to the author. The correction also replaces an informal claim that Adian’s procedure may loop with the more precise statement that it may fail to terminate. An infinite computation need not repeat a previously seen state.','correction'),
 paragraph('There is concrete evidence that short relations can require very long equality proofs. Nyberg-Brodda constructs the family ⟨a,b | baa(ba)^N=a⟩ for N≥2 with at least exponential Dehn function. Here the Dehn function measures the maximum, over equal word pairs of bounded total input length, of the minimum number of relation applications needed to connect them. Nevertheless, the same paper proves that every monoid in this family has decidable word problem.','dehn'),
 paragraph('Gray and Reilly’s August 2026 paper proves the existence of a two-generator one-relator inverse monoid with undecidable word problem. Inverse monoid presentations are interpreted in a different algebraic theory that includes a generalized inverse operation and its identities. One displayed relation in that theory is not one ordinary string relation in the present sense. The paper’s introduction explicitly retains the ordinary-monoid problem and the relevant reduced-relator inverse-monoid case as open.','inverse'),
 paragraph('The inverse-monoid connection is still useful: positive decidability results for the appropriate intermediate class would imply positive results for ordinary one-relation monoids. A negative result in a broader intermediate class need not transfer backwards through that implication. The fundamental goal remains an exact boundary for algorithmic equality under extremely small presentations, with consequences for rewriting systems, normal forms and the ways finite algebraic syntax can encode unbounded computation.')],
 why='A foundational decidability boundary for rewriting: can reachability under one literal reversible string rule already escape every terminating uniform algorithm?',
 importance=dict(score=96,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='One of the central minimal-presentation decision problems, connecting string rewriting, algebraic normal forms, computability and reductions between monoids, inverse monoids and groups.'),
 progress=[
 step('1932 and later','Magnus solves the one-relator group word problem; later work solves major ordinary-monoid special cases, without providing a general procedure.','survey'),
 step('2021-08-10','Nyberg-Brodda surveys the unresolved problem, reduction methods, special cases and gaps in earlier claimed arguments.','survey'),
 step('2022-09-07','The survey’s corrigendum fixes historical attributions and technical wording, including nontermination versus literal looping of Adian’s procedure.','correction'),
 step('2022-10-28','Nyberg-Brodda gives monadic one-relation monoids with exponential Dehn functions but decidable word problems, separating short derivations from decidability.','dehn'),
 step('2026-08-05','Gray–Reilly prove undecidability for certain two-generator one-relator inverse monoids; their introduction explicitly leaves the ordinary-monoid question open.','inverse')],
 references=[
 ref('survey','The word problem for one-relation monoids: a survey','Carl-Fredrik Nyberg-Brodda',2021,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-021-10216-8','Published 10 August; Semigroup Forum 103, 297–355; definitions, classical positive cases, compression and Adian’s procedure; read together with the 2022 correction; inherited source'),
 ref('correction','Correction to: The word problem for one-relation monoids: a survey','Carl-Fredrik Nyberg-Brodda',2022,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-022-10310-5','Published 7 September; Semigroup Forum 105, 834–838; items (8) and (10) correct nontermination terminology and the explicit problem’s historical attribution'),
 ref('dehn','On the Dehn functions of a class of monadic one-relation monoids','Carl-Fredrik Nyberg-Brodda',2022,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.16123','v1, 28 October; Theorem 1 gives exponential Dehn functions for ⟨a,b | baa(ba)^N=a⟩; Theorem 2 proves decidability for these examples'),
 ref('inverse','The word problem for two-generator one-relator inverse monoids','Robert D. Gray; Catherine Reilly',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04650','v1, 5 August; Theorem 1.1 gives inverse-monoid undecidability; introduction distinguishes the remaining ordinary-monoid and reduced-relator cases')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The August 2026 primary paper explicitly retains the ordinary one-relation-monoid word problem as open. Its inverse-monoid undecidability theorem does not resolve this ordinary presentation problem. Checked through 11 September 2026. The inherited input includes the presentation, so the card explicitly targets uniform decidability and distinguishes it from the per-presentation quantifier.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
